<%# 017 FR-001/FR-002: one axis of the floor filter. Rendered twice — once for the
    floor people are looking for, once for the floor they are on — from the same
    partial, because both behave identically.

    A <nav> with its own aria-label, so the two axes are two distinguishable
    landmarks rather than one undifferentiated pile of links (FR-021). The label
    text is the list's own column heading, per FR-002.

    Links, not a <select>: FR-008 requires that moving through the choices without
    committing to one leaves the list alone, and a select submitting on `change`
    fires as a keyboard user arrows through its options. A link is activated
    deliberately and never merely by being focused, so both halves of FR-008 hold
    by construction — with no JavaScript. %>
<% filter = filters[axis] %>
<nav id="locker-wish-filter-<%= axis.to_s.dasherize %>" class="filter-group" aria-label="<%= label %>">
  <span class="filter-label" aria-hidden="true"><%= label %></span>

  <%# FR-003: always offered, and the state the screen opens in. aria-current is
      what marks the choice in force — the styling hangs off that same attribute,
      so the state cannot be shown without also being announced (FR-015). %>
  <%= link_to t("locker_wishes.floor_filter.all_floors"), locker_wish_filter_path(filters, axis, nil),
        class: "filter-choice",
        aria: { current: ("true" unless filter.filtering?) } %>

  <%# FR-007 order, and FR-015/FR-020: #choices keeps a selection that no wish
      carries any more, so the viewer can always see and undo what is in force. %>
  <% filter.choices.each do |floor| %>
    <%= link_to floor, locker_wish_filter_path(filters, axis, floor),
          class: "filter-choice",
          aria: { current: ("true" if filter.current?(floor)) } %>
  <% end %>
</nav>
